issue.body:
I'm having some issues with the `ReflectionContainer` and autowiring. I tried digging into the code to do a PR, but I could not really figure out the problem _yet_.

In my example, I share a specific instance of a class first:

``` php
$container = new League\Container\Container;

$container->delegate(
    new League\Container\ReflectionContainer
);

$container->share('App\Client', function()
{
    return new App\Client('test', 'test');
});
```

And I have another class called `App\SDK` which requires a `App\Client` instance from the constructor. Here comes the bad part. When I try to create an instance of `App\SDK` using reflection, I'm getting a lot of errors:

``` php
$container->get('App\SDK');

PHP Fatal error:  Uncaught exception 'League\Container\Exception\NotFoundException' with message 'Unable to resolve a value for parameter (username) in the function/method (__construct)' in .../vendor/league/container/src/Argument/ArgumentResolverTrait.php:66
```

It seems like it tries to create the `App\Client` even though I have already bound an instance of it to the container. While digging into the code, I have noticed that `$this->getContainer()` returns `NULL` in the `ArgumentResolverTrait`. Shouldn't this contain the original container instance?

In version 2.x it was possible to overwrite previously added services on the container. Since 3.x appends definitions to the internal definitions array, the first definition added will always be returned.

Are there plans to bring this back to 3.x so that this is possible?

```
$container->add(Foo::class, $foo);
$container->add(Foo::class, $bar);

$bar === $container->get(Foo::class);
```

This is useful when doing functional tests when you boot your application+container and overwrite certain container definitions with mocks.

When adding the same service provider to a container, the service provider is added to the aggregate `providers` array regardless of whether it has already been added.

A use case would be 2 bootable service providers that have an internal dependency (add) a common service provider.

`Provider1->boot()` adds `Provider0`
`Provider2->boot()` adds `Provider0`

After `Provider1` and `Provider2` are added to the aggregate, `Provider0` exists in `providers` twice.

`Provider0` will only ever be registered once, but any `boot` method will be called each time it is added.

Additionally when resolving a dependency _not_ provided by `Provider0`, the redundant `Provider0` aggregates may be iterated.

The simplest fix would be to check the existence of the provider when adding
```php
    public function add($provider) : ServiceProviderAggregateInterface
    {
        if (is_string($provider) && $this->getContainer()->has($provider)) {
            $provider = $this->getContainer()->get($provider);
        } elseif (is_string($provider) && class_exists($provider)) {
            $provider = new $provider;
        }

        // return if provider has already been aggregated
        if (in_array($provider->getIdentifier(), $this->providers)) {
            return $this;
        }
```

However I wanted to understand if this behavior was intentional before submitting a PR.

Cheers,
Lee
